WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  beirut was expecting tension but nothing  like this  for hours hezbollah fighters fought with  gunmen the civilians ran for cover  in a nearby school children were told to  lie face down for safety  it was a scene of masked men snipers  rocket-propelled grenades and bodies on  the street  the children were outside during recess  and we moved them immediately to safe  places we moved them to lower levels and  then we waited until the parents came to  take their children of course the  children were going through panic and  fear they cried and screamed  the lebanese armed forces moved in as  the casualties were taken out it  literally feels like a war zone here  in beirut today uh residents in areas  nearby where the clashes are happening  are  terrorized they are trapped between  armed gunmen on both sides  the violence broke out as hezbollah and  its closest political ally were marching  to protest against the judge who's  conducting an inquiry into last year's  beirut port explosion  there has still been no one held  accountable for the blast  lebanon has been in collapse for months  it is experiencing one of the worst  financial crises in history  fuel shortages mean people have to queue  for hours to fill up vehicles and jerry  cans  basic medicine is often hard to come by  and state electricity is rationed to an  hour or two a day leaving major cities  in almost total darkness come nightfall  this is a worrying moment for lebanon  the scenes of gun battles on the streets  of beirut  are reminiscent of the civil war of the  1970s and 80s lebanon is already  teetering on collapse it wouldn't take  much to tip it over the edge  the coming hours will be crucial  will the violence escalate all can't be  restored  lebanon's future is in the balance  alistar bunker sky news

